# The Mutilated God
Tamil title: மூளியானார்

"The great sinner's skull will shatter into a thousand pieces. Watch, watch; it will happen within a second.

"The earth will split and swallow this sinner whole. Watch.

"The sea will surge and a fierce cyclone will arise. It will not stop with destroying the culprit; it will ruin this entire country for having tolerated him."

The old muttered; women cursed; young people stood open-mouthed. Everyone looked back and forth between the youth and the god's statue he had mutilated. Many were filled with terror.

They had worshipped that statue. Before the devotees' very eyes, the youth struck it forcefully and mutilated it.

"Look at these forms, these useless forms! Is this stone statue a god? Fools, is this stone shape a god that will protect you? Let protecting you wait. First let this god protect itself!"

He shouted in this manner and, while the people watched in bewilderment, broke and mutilated the strange god enshrined in the market square. They saw the broken statue and saw that no calamity occurred. They saw the youth who had committed this supposedly sacrilegious deed stand without the slightest fear in the market square and pour out words condemning that strange deity.

Generation after generation they had worshipped this god. Now they saw it mutilated and looked on in agitation.

He laughed aloud and mocked them: "A helpless god! You have been closing your eyes, folding your hands, and worshipping it. Have you now seen it mutilated?"

The common people had believed that only by praising and worshipping countless gods could one's birth become fruitful; that dawn and dusk, the sea not overflowing, the sky not collapsing, and the earth not splitting open and releasing a great fire to destroy everything were all "his" work; and that only daily worship would make "him" preserve their lives. Educated men wrote authoritative books supporting this and became "intellectuals."

Sun Yat-sen detested this foolishness. Radical thought and the teaching of Christian missionaries combined to shape his mind.

He explained to his country's people, who lived and died in fear, that the theism they trusted and praised was a meaningless storehouse of obscenity. They tried to stop him.

One day he went to the market square. There he found Chinese people, as usual, bowing and worshipping a strange deity. "Can this stone form, which lacks the ability even to protect itself, protect you?" he asked. Then he broke and mutilated the statue.

It was the festival day of the market-square deity, so devotees were present in great numbers and the priests were in full celebration. In these circumstances Sun Yat-sen mutilated the idol.

He was sixteen years old then. In 1882, Sun Yat-sen, who had been born into an orthodox family, lived in a home containing worship, fasts, vows, and everything found in other Chinese homes. Sun Yat-sen called it foolishness. The orthodox called the youth mad.

The Chinese people who were horrified that day when he mutilated the statue have today abandoned the strange god of the market square and celebrate Dr Sun Yat-sen as the foremost leader who brought China a revolution of new knowledge.

Dr Sun Yat-sen's life contains many incidents that captivate the heart. The incident of the broken idol, on that day, was especially stirring.

"Democracy must be established. Foreign domination must be completely abolished. The standard of living of our people must rise!"

These were the three principles held by the Chinese leader Dr Sun Yat-sen, whose revolution triumphed in 1911.

The task he undertook was immense.

"I shall never forget the young shepherd women of this land filled with flower gardens and almond trees, the most beautiful in the world; the mansions and towers overflowing with enchantment; or the silk garments that made the lovely women of this country shine."

Thus wrote Marco Polo, the Italian traveller who journeyed to China on foot and returned in the thirteenth century.

But the China Sun Yat-sen saw was an abode of sorrow and a well of suffering.

Of China's population of more than four hundred million, over three hundred million depended upon agriculture. The rivers either poured out floods or spread sand. There was either excessive rain or drought. Famine always raged in some part of the country. Locusts added to the misery by preventing the harvest of ripened crops. Hundreds of thousands died of famine every year. Still more departed the world through disease.

The government imposed upon a country in this condition taught cruelty its grammar. The landlords who strengthened it stood as symbols of exploitation.

To crown all this, the European powers plundered China itself. Ship after ship carried away China's natural products, silk and tea, along with porcelain made from its clay. In return, the Europeans gave the people opium.

When men such as Sun Yat-sen awakened and rose, China was a colony of opium addicts. Wearing a long queue, the Chinese man lay lost to the world in opium. The white man rolled in smiles amid the profits produced by his labour. China's royal dynasty and landlords submitted to this and enjoyed its benefits.

The revolution begun by Sun Yat-sen abolished the royal dynasty and allowed democracy to blossom. But white imperial powers prevented the next stage.

As soon as the Great War ended, the Western countries incited Japan against China. In a monkey's settlement, they handed Chinese territories to Japan. France and Britain sought regions for themselves as well. To assist this, they brought China's lineage of nobles under their control...

---NOTES---
The title மூளியானார் refers ironically to the deity rendered broken or mutilated by Sun Yat-sen. The source OCR reads 1982, but the surrounding statement that he was sixteen and the historical chronology resolve the year as 1882. The final scan ends mid-sentence during the account of Western imperial manipulation in China.
